ods associated with that deployment. If the deployment isn\'t available, the pods might be in a crash loop or have errors. So, using kubectl to get the pods for the recommendation deployment would help. The command would be "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" filtered for the recommendation service. \n\nAlternatively, checking the deployment\'s details with "kubectl describe deployment recommendation -n hotel-reservation" could provide more insights into why it\'s not scaling up. But first, checking the pods directly might show if they\'re crashing or not running. Also, looking at the logs of the pods could reveal specific errors. But since the user can only use the provided tools, the next logical step is to check the pods related to the failing deployment.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `recommendation` deployment is showing `0/1` replicas available, indicating a critical failure. To diagnose this, I need to inspect the associated pods directly. The command `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation` will list all pods in the namespace, allowing me to identify if the `recommendation` service has pods in a crash loop, pending state, or other anomalies. This will help pinpoint whether the issue is with the deployment configuration, pod startup failures, or resource constraints.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 362, 'prompt_tokens': 3195, 'total_tokens': 3557, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd94-9cc2-7ed2-a37a-fe8135c02c80-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3195, 'output_tokens': 362, 'total_tokens': 3557}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bb61e241-a9e6-4117-90bb-1fcdc31f4486'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose the Hotel Reservation application, which has multiple microservices. So far, they've checked the services and deployments using kubectl. The key issue found was the 'recommendation' deployment showing 0/1 available replicas. That means the deployment isn't running any pods, which is a problem.\n\nThe next step is to check the pods in the hotel-reservation namespace. By running 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation', they can see the current state of all pods. This will help determine if the recommendation service's pods are crashing, pending, or not created at all. \n\nLooking at the previous outputs, the services and deployments were listed, but the pods weren't checked yet. The user needs to see the pod details to understand why the deployment isn't working. If the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, there might be an application error or a configuration issue. If it's Pending, there could be resource constraints or image pull issues. \n\nSo, the logical next tool call is to get the list of pods in the namespace. That will provide the necessary information to proceed with further diagnostics. Once they have the pod statuses, they can take the next steps, like checking logs or events if there are issues.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a3ec9c4d7d0bdb1d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 296, 'prompt_tokens': 4213, 'total_tokens': 4509, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd94-ae28-72d3-a75e-8c4bb3