 port 9100 is already in use on all nodes. To confirm, I need to check each node\'s port usage. \n\nBut how? The available tools don\'t include a direct way to check port usage. However, the user can use `kubectl get nodes` to list the nodes, then for each node, run `kubectl describe node <node-name>` to see if port 9100 is in use. But the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run `kubectl get nodes` and `kubectl describe node`. \n\nWait, the user can first get the list of nodes with `kubectl get nodes -n hotel-reservation` to find the node names. Then, for each node, run `kubectl describe node <node-name>` to check the allocated ports. However, the current tools don\'t allow for multiple commands. The user can only run one command per tool call. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is that the host port 9100 is not available. The pod\'s host port is set to 9100, but if that port is already in use on all nodes, the pod can\'t be scheduled. The solution would be to change the host port to an unused one. \n\nBut how to confirm that? The user can check the node\'s port usage. However, with the current tools, the best approach is to check the node\'s allocated ports. So, the next step is to get the list of nodes and then describe each node to check port usage. But since the user can only make one tool call at a time, the next step would be to get the list of nodes. \n\nWait, the user already has the list of services, but not the nodes. So, the next tool call should be `kubectl get nodes -n hotel-reservation` to get the node names. Then, for each node, run `kubectl describe node <node-name>` to check the port allocations. But since the user can only make one tool call per step, the next step is to get the nodes first. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user can check the node\'s port usage via the `kubectl describe node` command. But without knowing the node names, it\'s not possible. So, the next step is to get the list of nodes. \n\nWait, the user hasn\'t run `kubectl get nodes` yet. So, the next tool call should be `kubectl get nodes -n hotel-reservation` to list the nodes. Then, for each node, check the port usage. But the user can only make one tool call at a time. So, the next step is to get the nodes.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `recommendation` pod is pending due to port conflicts on all nodes (host port 9100 is occupied). To confirm this, I need to check the node\'s allocated ports. First, I\'ll retrieve the list of nodes in the namespace to identify which nodes are available. This will allow me to inspect each node\'s port usage and verify if port 9100 is already in use, which would explain the scheduling failure.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get nodes -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=1250, prompt_tokens=4852, total_tokens=6102, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd94-e2ec-77c0-9a29-7f7ea5f5c51b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4852, 'output_tokens': 1250, 'total_tokens': 6102}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8a42ade1-339a-44c4-8d12-5e58690d8a17')]]
