dation\' service isn\'t running. The user needs to check the pods associated with that deployment. If the deployment isn\'t available, the pods might be in a crash loop or have errors. So, using kubectl to get the pods for the recommendation deployment would help. The command would be "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" filtered for the recommendation service. \n\nAlternatively, checking the deployment\'s details with "kubectl describe deployment recommendation -n hotel-reservation" could provide more insights into why it\'s not scaling up. But first, checking the pods directly might show if they\'re crashing or not running. Also, looking at the logs of the pods could reveal specific errors. But since the user can only use the provided tools, the next logical step is to check the pods related to the failing deployment.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `recommendation` deployment is showing `0/1` replicas available, indicating a critical failure. To diagnose this, I need to inspect the associated pods directly. The command `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation` will list all pods in the namespace, allowing me to identify if the `recommendation` service has pods in a crash loop, pending state, or other anomalies. This will help pinpoint whether the issue is with the deployment configuration, pod startup failures, or resource constraints.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 362, 'prompt_tokens': 3195, 'total_tokens': 3557, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd94-9cc2-7ed2-a37a-fe8135c02c80-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3195, 'output_tokens': 362, 'total_tokens': 3557}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call acc