try with the tools.", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9ffcc26f-ba60-4b5f-910b-38ef901db21b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: rollback_command\n\ntool description: Use this function to roll back the last kubectl command you observed\nWhen you call this tool multiple times, you will roll-back previous commands in the reverse order of their prior execution."\n\n\ntool name: get_previous_rollbackable_cmd\n\ntool description: Use this function to get a list of commands you previously executed that could be roll-backed.\n\n\ntool name: r_submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit after you rolled back all the changes.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b9f74fb9-e9c6-47fb-8c91-f3ce3dce0c90'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is asking me to rollback changes in an IT environment, specifically using Kubernetes and SRE tools. The main goal is to revert the system to its initial state by undoing the last operations. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out which tool to use. The available tools are wait_tool, rollback_command, get_previous_rollbackable_cmd, and r_submit_tool. \n\nThe user mentioned that the rollback tool undoes the last operation, and they might need to run it multiple times. But to do that, I should first check what commands are available to rollback. The get_previous_rollbackable_cmd tool seems useful here because it lists the commands that can be rolled back. Without knowing which commands were executed, I can't effectively use rollback_command. \n\nSo, the first step should be to retrieve the list of previous commands using get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. Once I have that list, I can determine the order and which commands to undo. However, the user's initial instruction says to run the rollback tool as many times as possible until there are no more operations to undo. But if I don't know the commands, maybe I should start by getting the list. \n\nWait, but the rollback_command can be called multiple times to undo previous commands in reverse order. However, if there are no commands to rollback, it might not do anything. So maybe the correct approach is to first check the available commands. But the user's task says to rollback until no more operations are left. So perhaps the initial step is to use get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to find out which commands are available. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the rollback_command can be called directly, but if there are no commands, it would just exit. But the user wants to ensure that all changes are rolled back. So the safest approach is to first get the list of commands that can be rolled back. That way, I can process them in the correct order. \n\nWait, but the user's instruction says to run the rollback tool as many times as possible. So maybe the first step is to call rollback_command repeatedly until it can't anymore. But if there are no commands, it would stop. However, without knowing the commands, maybe the system has some history. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_previous_rollbackable_cmd is necessary to list the commands, then use rollback_command on each. But the user's task says to run the rollback tool (which is rollback_command) as many times as possible. So perhaps the correct first step is to call get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to get the list, then iterate through them. But the user's current step is the t