\'t found, maybe there\'s another service or a different configuration. Alternatively, maybe the pod is using a hostPort without a corresponding service, leading to the conflict. \n\nThe next step should be to check all services in the namespace to see if any are using port 9100. But the user already tried getting the services and didn\'t find \'recommendation\' as a service. Maybe the service is named differently or isn\'t a Kubernetes Service. Alternatively, the user could check the node\'s port usage directly, but that\'s not possible with the current tools. \n\nWait, the user has the get_services tool, which lists services from Grafana. The previous response showed that \'recommendation\' is in the list, but when trying to get the service YAML, it\'s not found. That\'s confusing. Maybe the service is not a Kubernetes Service but a different type of service in Grafana. Or perhaps the service was deleted, and the deployment is still referencing it. \n\nAlternatively, the user might need to check the pod\'s YAML again to see if there\'s a hostPort specified. The previous deployment YAML showed the containerPort as 8085, but the pod might have a hostPort set in the pod spec. Wait, in the deployment YAML, the container\'s ports are defined with containerPort: 8085, but the pod\'s hostPort isn\'t mentioned there. So maybe the hostPort is set in the pod\'s spec, which isn\'t part of the deployment. \n\nWait, the user\'s initial error was about the pod being pending due to host port 9100 conflict. So the pod\'s configuration must have a hostPort set to 9100. But in the deployment YAML, the containerPort is 8085. That suggests that the pod\'s spec might have a hostPort set, which isn\'t visible in the deployment YAML. Maybe the pod is configured with a hostPort in the pod spec, which isn\'t part of the deployment. \n\nSo the user needs to check the pod\'s YAML directly. The previous command to get the deployment didn\'t show the hostPort, but the pod\'s YAML might have it. The user should run `kubectl get pod -n hotel-reservation -o yaml` to check the pod\'s configuration. \n\nBut the user can\'t do that because they don\'t have the pod\'s name. Wait, the initial error mentioned the pod \'recommendation\' is pending. So the pod name might be \'recommendation\'. So the user should run `kubectl get pod recommendation -n hotel-reservation -o yaml` to check the pod\'s spec for hostPort. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the pod\'s YAML to see if there\'s a hostPort set. If the pod is using hostPort 9100, that\'s the root cause. The solution would be to change the hostPort in the pod\'s configuration or ensure that no other service is using that port. \n\nSince the user can\'t get the service YAML, maybe the service isn\'t a Kubernetes Service but a different type, or the user needs to check the pod\'s configuration directly. The next step is to get the pod\'s YAML to confirm the hostPort setting.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The `recommendation` service was not found in the Kubernetes cluster, which suggests that the pod is not exposed via a service and is directly using a host port (9100). To confirm this, I need to inspect the pod\'s YAML configuration to check if `hostPort: 9100` is explicitly set in the container\'s port definition. This will clarify whether the port conflict is due to a direct pod configuration or an unregistered service. \n\nNext, I will generate the tool call to execute this action.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1048, 'prompt_tokens': 4895, 'total_tokens': 5943, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd98-4b1f-7380-9386-4b8f94841712-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_token